        "reported_material_gaps": [],
        "research_rationale": "Identity: the census register lists EO 6875 dated 1934-10-16 (Franklin D. Roosevelt); the WPA 1944 list entry for 1934 Oct 16, p.593, records the code for the Silk Textile Industry as approved, and the held text's date and subject agree. APP page check passed: the mirror page record with the held text's sha256 (common-crawl capture) has a heading naming this order's number, the same date and subject, and a text_sha256 equal to the held text's. The held text was read in full. Own terms: see scope check. Repeal route per the reviewed shared analysis: an approval under section 3 depended on the code approval and enforcement provisions that section 2 of S.J. Res. 113 repealed on June 14, 1935; Schechter (May 27, 1935) is recorded only as a validity characterization.\nMember checks: {\"earlier_end_check\":\"No held record shows this code cancelled, modified under section 10(b) in a way that replaces it, suspended or superseded before June 14, 1935: the census register, the WPA 1944 list and a code-name search of the held order texts (jobs/*/output/texts) were checked, and the machine searches (current-law:usc, current-law:ecfr, later-events:ofr_catalog) have no hit for this order; the OFR catalog starts in 1937 and is not adequate alone, and a code-name search can miss an order that names the code in other words. Held EO 6330 is the original approval of the Silk Textile Industry code (an earlier act, not an end). Held EO 6878 (October 16, 1934) and EO 6930 (December 27, 1934) act under \\\"the Codes of Fair Competition for the Cotton Textile Industry, the Silk Textile Industry and the Wool Textile Industry\\\" and so treat the code as in force; neither cancels, suspends or supersedes it. June 16, 1935 is not an end: section 1 of S.J. Res. 113 replaced the two-year expiry with April 1, 1936, and nothing in the resolution defers section 2.\",\"independent_command_check\":\"No appointment, delegation, committee, report direction or other command independent of the code appears; \\\"Approval recommended: Hugh S. Johnson, Administrator\\\" is a recommendation, not a command.\",\"savings_check\":\"Savings source SUPPLEMENT:SJRES113 (with SUPPLEMENT:NIRA5 for the section 5 text) read; accrued acts, liabilities and the section 5 exemption for completed action survive only as accrued effects; no held source shows a prosecution or enforcement continued after June 14, 1935, and none continues this approval.\",\"scope_check\":\"The order has one operative command, the approval of the code: \\\"do hereby order that said amendment be and it hereby is approved, and that previous approval of the amended portions of the Code is hereby modified to include an approval of said portions of the Code in their entirety as amended.\\\" The inventory is a single whole-order clause and was confirmed against the full text; the recitals describe the application, hearings and the Administrator's findings and command nothing. The one sentence has two parts, approval of the amendment and modification of the previous approval of the amended portions to include them \\\"in their entirety as amended\\\"; both are code approval acts that depend on the code and end together. The recital about the Board of Inquiry for the Cotton Textile Industry (Executive Order No. 6840) states why the amendment was made and commands nothing.\"}",
